{
  "dom_id": "question-167589",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Find $m,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=k(x)$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $m=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We have $k(x)=3{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}-2x.$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is equal to ${k}^{′}(1).$ From step 6, the derivative is\n\n$$\n{k}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=1$ and get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{′}(1) & =3{e}^{1}-3(1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =3e-3-2 \\\\  & =3e-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $3e-5.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
